# Case Analysis: Kwok Cheuk Kin and Another v Director of Lands and Others [2021] HKCA 54

## 1. Summary of Facts

This case concerned a constitutional challenge to Hong Kong's **New Territories Small House Policy** ("Small House Policy" or "Ding Rights"), an administrative policy in force since December 1972. Under the policy, a male indigenous villager of the New Territories ("NTII") — defined as a male person at least 18 years old descended through the **male line** from a resident in 1898 of a recognized village — may apply, **once in his lifetime**, for permission to build a "small house" (a three-storey building not exceeding 27 ft in height and 700 sq ft in roofed-over area) within his own village environs.

Small house grants take three forms:
1. **Free building licence** — on private land already owned by the applicant, at nil premium;
2. **Private treaty grant** — direct grant of Government land at a concessionary premium (about 2/3 of market rate);
3. **Land exchange** — surrender and re-grant of land at nil or reduced premium.

Two applicants (Kwok Cheuk Kin and another) brought judicial review proceedings challenging the constitutionality of the policy on three grounds:
- It discriminates against **non-indigenous** persons on grounds of social origin/birth, contrary to articles 25 and 39 of the Basic Law ("BL25", "BL39") and article 22 of the Hong Kong Bill of Rights ("BOR22");
- It discriminates against **female** indigenous villagers on grounds of sex, contrary to the same provisions (and the Sex Discrimination Ordinance exemption was also challenged);
- The Government breached its duty under **BL7** to manage land for the benefit of all Hong Kong residents.

The respondents (Director of Lands and others) and the interested party (the Heung Yee Kuk, representing indigenous villagers) defended the policy by invoking **Article 40 of the Basic Law ("BL40")**, which provides: *"The lawful traditional rights and interests of the indigenous inhabitants of the 'New Territories' shall be protected by the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region."*

At first instance, Chow J found the policy inherently discriminatory (which was conceded), but held that only the **free building licence** component was protected by BL40 as a "lawful traditional right"; the **private treaty grant** and **exchange** components failed his "tracing exercise" back to pre-1898 rights and were declared unconstitutional. All parties appealed.

## 2. Summary of the Parties' Arguments

### The Applicants (Appellants in part)
Led by Mr Martin Lee SC, the applicants argued:
- **"Lawful" in BL40** means lawful according to Hong Kong law *including* the Basic Law itself — so a right that discriminates contrary to BL25, BL39 and BOR22 cannot be "lawful" and thus cannot be protected by BL40. On this view, the *entire* Small House Policy should have been struck down.
- Alternatively, even the free building licence is not protected because:
  - The Ding Rights are not "rights or interests" at all — the policy merely confers a right to *apply* under a wholly discretionary government scheme;
  - The Ding Rights are not "traditional" — the free building licence has nothing in common with pre-1898 practices except that it is free of premium.
- They also supported the Judge's "tracing exercise" approach, under which private treaty grant and exchange failed to qualify.
- They also sought to rely on a "double proportionality" analysis (leave to amend was refused).

### The Respondents and Interested Party
Led by Mr Benjamin Yu SC (respondents) and Lord Pannick QC (interested party), they argued:
- The **entirety** of the Ding Rights under the Small House Policy — including private treaty grant and exchange — falls within BL40 and is constitutionally protected.
- **On "traditional":** The Judge erred in requiring a tracing exercise back to 1898. BL40 protects the rights and interests enjoyed by NTIIs at the time of the Basic Law's drafting (and immediately prior to its commencement), reflecting the Basic Law's overarching theme of **continuity** after the resumption of sovereignty.
- **On "lawful":** The word is merely *descriptive* of those traditional rights and interests recognized as lawful in the legal system before the Basic Law came into force; it does not import a requirement that the rights survive challenge under BL25/BOR22. The drafters knew the policy was discriminatory and deliberately entrenched it anyway.
- The Basic Law must be read as a coherent whole: BL40, BL120 and BL122 (which preserve small house rent concessions for male-line descendants) together demonstrate that all forms of small house grants were intended to continue.
- **On relief:** In any event, the judicial review should have been dismissed for the applicants' **lack of standing** and their **substantial, unexplained delay** (the policy dated from 1972; the Basic Law had been in force for 23 years).

## 3. Legal Principles (Ratio Decidendi)

### (a) The contextual and purposive approach to construing the Basic Law

The Court reaffirmed that provisions of the Basic Law are construed contextually and purposively (*Ng Ka Ling*; *Chong Fung Yuen*; *Vallejos*; *Comilang*). The context and purpose are established at the time of enactment (April 1990), and admissible extrinsic materials include the Joint Declaration, the state of legislation at the relevant time, and pre-enactment/drafting materials. Drafting materials are relevant not as evidence of drafters' subjective intentions, but as part of the legislative history informing the provision's purpose (following the Australian approach in *Cole v Whitfield* and *Brownlee*).

Because BL40 concerns "traditional" rights, the Court held that a **historical survey back to the New Territories Lease of 1898** was necessary to establish the special factual context — but, crucially, this historical survey served to establish *context and purpose*, not to define the temporal scope of the rights protected.

### (b) The construction of "lawful traditional rights and interests" in BL40 — the central holding

This is the heart of the judgment, and it departed significantly from the approach of the court below.

**On "traditional":** The Court rejected the Judge's "tracing exercise" as the *definitive* test. While tradition by definition has historical origins, the Court held that whether a right is "traditional" for BL40 purposes is determined **by reference to the state of affairs in April 1990** (when the Basic Law was promulgated), not by whether the right can be traced in substance to pre-1898 practices. The Court reasoned:

- The term "NTII" itself only came into existence in 1898, so the drafters necessarily had that date in mind as the *origin* of the status — but the protection was aimed at rights *extant* at the time of drafting.
- Human experience shows traditional rights **evolve** as they pass between generations, and may suffer "occasional gaps and disruptions" without losing continuity. The drafters must have intended to protect such rights provided they **retain their essential or core features**. This accords with the principle that constitutional provisions must be interpreted to protect the *essence* of the right from impairment (*W v Registrar of Marriages*).
- The Court expressly cautioned that *Tse Kwan Sang* and *Koon Ping Leung* — earlier cases that had supported a tracing approach — should **not be regarded as authoritative** on the "traditional" element, because the full historical and drafting materials were not before the courts in those cases.

**On "lawful":** The Court held that "lawful" means those rights and interests which are, **as a matter of historical fact, recognized to be the NTIIs' lawful traditional rights and interests in the Hong Kong legal system at the time of the promulgation of the Basic Law on 4 April 1990**. It does *not* mean:
- lawful under Qing law before 1898 (rejecting the applicants); nor
- lawful according to domestic law on 1 July 1997 tested against the Basic Law (rejecting the Judge's formulation in a way that would leave lawfulness open to post-hoc judicial determination); nor
- a free-standing requirement that the right survive constitutional challenge.

The Court reasoned that if "lawful" were left to be determined by courts after 1997 by reference to Qing law or later law, it would create uncertainty and render the constitutional promise in BL40 "inefficacious" — clearly not the drafters' intent. Applying the **theme of continuity**, BL40 *continues and elevates* the pre-existing recognition and protection of such rights to a constitutional level.

**The consequence — BL40 as a constitutional shield against equality challenges:** Because the Ding Rights were recognized as lawful traditional rights in the legal system at the time of promulgation (despite heated contemporaneous debate about their discriminatory nature), they fall within BL40 and merit constitutional protection **in full, despite their inherently discriminatory nature**. BL40 "shields them from any challenge against their legality based on discrimination or other constitutional grounds including the equality provisions such as BL25, BL39 and BOR22." To allow such challenges would defeat the whole purpose of BL40. This is arguably the most controversial aspect of the ruling: two explicitly non-discrimination constitutional provisions (BL25, BL39/BOR22) are effectively *ousted* by BL40 in respect of the Ding Rights, without any proportionality balancing.

### (c) The coherence principle: BL40 read with BL120 and BL122

The Court invoked the principle that the Basic Law must be read as a **coherent whole** (*Comilang* (CFA)). Two applications:

**First, BL40 with BL120 and BL122:**
- **BL120** (implementing paragraph 1 of Annex III to the Joint Declaration) provides that all leases granted before the HKSAR's establishment extending beyond 30 June 1997, "*and all rights in relation to such leases*," continue to be recognized and protected. The Court held that "rights in relation to such leases" includes the right to apply for small house grants.
- **BL122** (implementing paragraph 2 of Annex III) preserves unchanged rent for small houses granted **after 30 June 1984** to male-line descendants of 1898 villagers — which necessarily extends to grants made *after* 1997. The rent benefit is predicated on the continued validity of small house grants after handover, and the male-line descent language is lifted directly from the Small House Policy.
- Reading the three provisions together, small house grants in **all forms**, whether before or after 30 June 1997, continue to be recognized and protected. Any contrary construction of BL40 "would render BL120 and BL122, insofar as small house grants are concerned, quite meaningless." The Court expressly held the Judge erred in failing to construe BL40 coherently with BL120 and BL122.

The Court also found support in **Annex III of the Joint Declaration** itself, holding (contrary to the Judge) that paragraphs 1 and 2 evidenced an agreement between the UK and PRC Governments to preserve and protect small house grants beyond 1 July 1997 — the rent concessions in paragraph 2 being "underpinned by the continued validity of small house grants."

**Second, BL40 with BL25, BL39 and BOR22 via the CEDAW reservations:** This is a novel and significant interpretive move. Both the UK and PRC Governments had entered **reservations to CEDAW** (in 1996 and 1997 respectively) stating that laws enabling male indigenous villagers to exercise certain property rights and rent concessions would continue to be applied. The Court held:

- These reservations are "a weighty aid to the interpretation of the Basic Law and the BOR" (following *Comilang* and *ZN v Secretary for Justice*), and BL40 must be construed consistently with them.
- The reservations render a discrimination challenge based on **sex** impermissible.
- By analogy with the Court of Final Appeal's reasoning in *Comilang* (where the immigration reservation in s.11 of the Hong Kong Bill of Rights Ordinance was held to extend by necessary implication to cognate Basic Law rights), the Court held that BL40 must also be construed to **exclude discrimination challenges based on other grounds, such as birth or social origin**. This ensures "a coherent constitutional scheme" protecting the Ding Rights.

The effect is that the CEDAW reservations — which on their face concerned only sex discrimination — are used to justify excluding *all* discrimination challenges to the Small House Policy.

### (d) The Ding Rights as "rights and interests"

Rejecting the argument that the policy confers only a discretionary right to apply, the Court held that a published government policy creates legal obligations on the Government under the doctrine of **legitimate expectation** (*Ng Siu Tung*) or the related principle of good administration (*R (Nadarajah)*; *Mandalia*). Correspondingly, an eligible NTII has a right to determination of his application in accordance with the policy — which plainly falls within "rights and interests" for BL40 purposes.

### (e) Alternative holding: even on the tracing approach, all three forms qualify

To complete the analysis, the Court assumed (contrary to its primary view) that the Judge's tracing exercise was required, and still reached the same conclusion:

- **Free building licence:** traceable (as the Judge found) — it captures the pre-1898 custom of building a house for one's own occupation on one's own land.
- **Private treaty grant:** the Judge erred in rejecting it. The Court traced the successive modes of disposing of village land — public auctions at concessionary upset prices (c.1901–1909), private treaty grants (1909 to post-WWII), restricted village auctions (1950s–1972, which were "private treaty grants in all but name," with District Officers going to great lengths to "cook the auctions"), and private treaty grants since 1972. The **concessionary rate** throughout was the significant unifying feature: it placed a villager without land in the same position as an owner of an Old Schedule lot applying for a free building licence. The essential core feature — an eligible villager's right to apply for new land to build a house at concessionary terms — was preserved throughout. The fact that women and non-villagers occasionally obtained land was dismissed as "anomalies."
- **Exchange:** merely a "derivative" of the free building licence — a practical means of overcoming difficulties (e.g., plots too small or awkwardly shaped) in exercising that right.

### (f) Relief: standing and delay (obiter but strongly stated)

Although the relief issue became academic, the Court stated it would have refused relief on two grounds, differing from the Judge:

- **Delay:** The delay was "not only serious... phenomenal" — the policy dated from 1972, the BOR from 1991, and the Basic Law had been in force 23 years, with "glaring" lack of explanation. The prejudice to third parties and detriment to good administration was substantial: between 1972 and 2018, 111,995 applications were received (8,657 still being processed), and many NTIIs had ordered their affairs on the policy's validity. The interest in good administration lies in consistent decisions made with reasonable dispatch and citizens knowing where they stand.
- **Standing:** The applicants had no interest beyond that of an ordinary resident — no intention to build a small house, no interest in any affected land, and no assertion that but for the policy they would have gained land or housing benefit.

### (g) Disposition

The appeals of the respondents and interested party were allowed; the applicants' appeal dismissed; the Judge's declarations of unconstitutionality (regarding private treaty grant, exchange, and the SDO exemption) were set aside; and the judicial review was dismissed in its entirety. **The Small House Policy is constitutional in its entirety.**

## 4. Striking, Unusual or Remarkable Aspects

Several features of this case are extraordinary:

**1. A constitutional right to sex discrimination.** The most striking aspect is that the Court of Appeal expressly held that a government policy that is *conceded* to be discriminatory on grounds of sex and social origin/birth — in breach of BL25, BL39 and BOR22 — is nevertheless fully constitutional because BL40 shields it. Hong Kong's constitution thus contains an express carve-out permitting, indeed protecting, discrimination against women and non-indigenous residents in land allocation. The Court achieved this without any proportionality analysis, and indeed refused the applicants leave to argue "double proportionality" on the basis that no competing rights needed balancing at all. The tension between BL40 and the equality guarantees is resolved not by balancing but by *subordination* of the latter.

**2. The "cooked auctions."** The historical evidence is remarkable: for two decades (1950s–1972), government officials deliberately manipulated supposedly public auctions — restricting publicity, holding them inside village offices or schools, withdrawing lots if non-villagers bid, and with villagers organizing among themselves so there was only one bidder per lot — all so that land would pass to villagers at nominal prices. The Court candidly described District Officers going to great lengths to "cook the auctions," and then treated this practice as evidence of an unbroken traditional right. One might think this was state-sanctioned allocation of public land to a privileged closed group; the Court saw it as continuity of custom.

**3. The 47-year delay and the standing point.** The Small House Policy was introduced in 1972 and survived unchallenged until 2019 — nearly half a century, including 28 years after the Bill of Rights came into force. The Court described the delay as "phenomenal." It is striking that a policy conferring valuable land benefits on a hereditary male class (defined by reference to who lived in a village in **1898**, over 120 years before the case) went constitutionally unexamined for so long in one of Asia's most developed legal systems.

**4. The definition frozen in 1898.** Eligibility depends on male-line descent from a person resident in an established village in 1898 — the year of the Second Convention of Peking. Constitutional rights in a modern financial centre thus turn on the residence of one's ancestors in the Qing dynasty, and pass exclusively through the male line. The Court's own historical survey details the pre-colonial customary tenure under which "traditionally, women could not inherit village land, whether arable or house-land, or houses" — a rule now effectively constitutionally entrenched.

**5. The judicial about-face on precedent.** The Court of Appeal expressly disavowed the tracing methodology that it (and lower courts) had previously endorsed in *Tse Kwan Sang* and *Koon Ping Leung*, on the ground that the full historical materials had not been presented in those cases. It is unusual for an intermediate appellate court to so candidly demote prior authority on a constitutional question of this magnitude — and notable that the judgment effectively invites (or dares) the Court of Final Appeal to confirm or correct it.

**6. The use of international treaty reservations to defeat domestic equality rights.** The interpretive move by which CEDAW reservations — concerned only with sex discrimination — were extended by analogy to immunize the policy against *all* discrimination challenges (including social origin/birth) is a bold and potentially far-reaching technique. It inverts the usual role of human rights treaties: instead of a lever for expanding equality, the reservation becomes a shield for entrenched discrimination.

**7. The scale of the stakes.** With 111,995 applications since 1972 and thousands pending, the case concerned the allocation of scarce land in one of the world's most expensive property markets to a constitutionally privileged hereditary male class — a policy the Court noted had never been legally challenged even after the Bill of Rights Ordinance 1991. The judgment ensures that this will continue, at least until the Court of Final Appeal or a constitutional amendment says otherwise. [end of text]


